WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 10: Greek Fire: The Ancient Byzantine Superweapon

1
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.960
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.240
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.440
[Rebecca] Throughout human history, great empires have risen and fallen on the strength of their technology.

4
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:19.920
[Rebecca] Few inventions in military history were as decisive, terrifying, and closely guarded as the legendary Byzantine superweapon, Greek fire.

5
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:29.760
[Rebecca] In the 7th century, the newly unified armies and navies of the Umayyad Caliphate swept across the Middle East, conquering Syria, Egypt, and North Africa.

6
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.200
[Rebecca] By 674, a colossal armada anchored off the Golden Gate of Constantinople.

7
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.240
[Rebecca] Western civilizations seemed poised to fall.

8
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.560
[Rebecca] What saved the Eastern Roman Empire was a secret chemical concoction that defied the laws of nature.

9
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:53.440
[Rebecca] A liquid fire that burned on top of the sea, ignited wooden ships in seconds, and turned enemy armadas into roaring floating crematoriums.

10
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.720
[George] Here is George with the story.

11
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:27.200
[George] Syria, Palestine, and the rich agricultural breadbasket of Roman Egypt had fallen in rapid succession, shattering the centuries-old geopolitical order of the Mediterranean basin.

12
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.520
[George] Now, the ambitious caliph Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufian set his sights upon the ultimate prize.

13
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:41.440
[George] Constantinople itself, the queen of cities, the beating heart of Christendom, and the impregnable capital of the Roman Empire.

14
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:53.760
[George] Muwiya assembled a colossal battle armada comprising hundreds of heavy war galleys, fast-raiding cruisers, and massive troop transports, sailing through the Hellespont to anchor off the Golden Gate.

15
00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:04.840
[George] To protect the inner harbor, Byzantine engineers stretched a colossal wrought iron boom chain across the entrance to the Golden Horn, anchored to massive stone towers on both shores.

16
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:16.360
[George] For five consecutive summers, from April to September, the Arab fleet maintained a suffocating naval blockade, launching relentless amphibious assaults against the colossal seawalls.

17
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:24.920
[George] The Byzantine navy, severely depleted by decades of brutal war with Sassanian Persia, was outnumbered at least three to one.

18
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:35.640
[George] Standing upon the marble ramparts of the great palace above the Sea of Marmara, the young Emperor Constantine IV watched the enemy fleet cover the waters from horizon to horizon.

19
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:43.720
[George] He knew with chilling certainty that conventional naval tactics and ramming maneuvers could never break the enemy's numerical superiority.

20
00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:56.920
[George] If the imperial capital fell, the overland and maritime gateway into the European continent would be flung wide open, altering the religious, linguistic, and cultural destiny of Western civilization forever.

21
00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:04.760
[George] Unless an asymmetrical technological miracle was uncovered, the Queen of Cities would be starved into unconditional surrender.

22
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:13.800
[George] Deliverance arrived in the empire's darkest hour in the person of an extraordinary Christian architect, engineer, and alchemist named Kalinikos.

23
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:25.880
[George] A native of Heliopolis, the ancient Greco-Roman temple city of Baalbek in the Bika Valley of modern-day Lebanon, Kalinikos had watched his ancestral homeland fall under Arab control.

24
00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:38.680
[George] Refusing to live under Islamic rule, he gathered his experimental treatises on chemical warfare and fled across the mountains to Constantinople, offering his services directly to Emperor Constantine IV.

25
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:46.480
[George] The emperor granted the refugee an immediate private audience, recognizing the profound military value of his chemical research.

26
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:58.320
[George] Constantine provided Kalinikos with an elite team of Imperial artisans, extensive funds, and a secure subterranean laboratory inside the imperial arsenal along the Golden Horn.

27
00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:09.520
[George] Working under strict armed guard behind double-locked iron doors, Kalinikos formulated a terrifying new incendiary liquid that would forever alter the history of naval warfare.

28
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:20.720
[George] While the precise recipe remained one of the most closely guarded secrets in human history, modern chemical historians believe the substance was a sophisticated petroleum-based compound.

29
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:33.600
[George] Kalinikos combined naturally occurring light crude oil, extracted from surface seeps around the Black Sea and the Kerch Peninsula, with distilled pine resin, volcanic sulfur, pitch, and quicklime.

30
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.800
[George] The chemical properties of this mixture were horrific.

31
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:44.560
[George] It was highly viscous, adhered stubbornly to wooden hull timbers and human flesh, and was completely impervious to water.

32
00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:52.080
[George] Most terrifying of all, the inclusion of quickly created an intense exothermic reaction upon contact with moisture.

33
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.800
[George] Throwing buckets of seawater onto Greek fire did not extinguish the blaze.

34
00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:04.640
[George] Instead, the water caused the boiling petroleum to spread rapidly across the waves, igniting in blinding sheets of fire.

35
00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:15.840
[George] The true technological genius of Greek fire lay not merely in its deadly chemistry, but in the sophisticated mechanical delivery system that Kalinikos and Byzantine engineers devised.

36
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:27.360
[George] The Imperial Navy retrofitted its fastest, most agile warships, two-tiered rowing cruisers known as Dramans, with specialized bronze flamethrowers mounted directly upon the forecastle.

37
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:36.280
[George] Deep in the hold of each draman, beneath the rowing benches, sat a sealed, heavy bronze cauldron suspended over an airtight charcoal furnace.

38
00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:44.280
[George] The thick petroleum liquid was poured into the cauldron and heated to near-boiling point, generating tremendous internal vapor pressure.

39
00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:56.520
[George] Specially trained marine engineers, clad in flame-resistant leather aprons, operated double-action brass force pumps, forcing the pressurized boiling liquid up through reinforced copper pipes.

40
00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:07.160
[George] On the forward deck, the pipe terminated in a swiveling bronze nozzle sculpted into the ferocious likeness of a roaring lion or dragon's head, intricately gilded in gold.

41
00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:15.400
[George] As the pressurized stream of boiling oil shot out of the beast's mouth, it passed through a hooded brazier containing a constant pilot flame.

42
00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:23.240
[George] The liquid ignited instantaneously in mid-air, hurling a roaring, fifty-foot continuous jet of liquid fire across the water.

43
00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:36.360
[George] The weapon produced an apocalyptic sensory experience, a thick, suffocating cloud of black sulphurous smoke and an earsplitting, thunderous roar that reverberated across the waves like rolling celestial thunder.

44
00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:43.960
[George] In the late summer of 677 AD, the decisive moment arrived on the choppy waters of the Sea of Marmara.

45
00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:54.440
[George] Believing the Byzantines were trapped within their harbors, the Arab armada formed a massive crescent formation, intending to surround and board the smaller Imperial squadron.

46
00:06:54.440 --> 00:07:08.600
[George] Suddenly, the iron gates of the Golden Horn swung open, the massive boom chain was dropped to the seabed, and Constantine IV's Dramans surged forward in tight wedge formation, their oars churning the sea into white foam.

47
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:14.840
[George] As the distance closed to forty feet, the bronze dragon heads on the Byzantine bows roared to life.

48
00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:21.760
[George] Jets of roaring liquid fire struck the wooden hulls, rigging and canvas sails of the leading Arab flagships.

49
00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:25.120
[George] What followed was absolute, unmitigated horror.

50
00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:32.480
[George] The sticky fire clung tenaciously to the pine planking, burning furiously even against the wet wood at the waterline.

51
00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:44.240
[George] Desperate Arab sailors drew buckets of seawater, throwing them onto the burning decks, only to watch in horror as the flames flared up violently, splattering boiling pitch across the crew.

52
00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:53.840
[George] Soldiers whose garments caught fire leaped into the sea to quench the flames, only to find the surface of the ocean covered in a carpet of floating, roaring fire.

53
00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:00.720
[George] Dozens of heavy war galleys were consumed in minutes, sending towering pillars of oily black smoke into the heavens.

54
00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:12.160
[George] The remaining Arab vessels broke formation in total panic, fleeing south towards Syria while Byzantine Dramans pursued them, leaving the sea choked with burning wreckage and floating embers.

55
00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:16.480
[George] The five-year siege was broken, and Constantinople was saved.

56
00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:24.320
[George] Greek fire proved to be the unbreakable shield that preserved the Byzantine Empire across half a millennium of existential warfare.

57
00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:37.520
[George] In 717 AD, the Umayyad Caliphate launched an even more staggering military invasion, an armada of 1800 ships transporting 80,000 men under Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik.

58
00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:43.600
[George] The newly crowned Emperor Leo Iai the Asaurian unleashed the imperial fireships once again.

59
00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:57.280
[George] In a single devastating afternoon, twenty heavy Arab transport vessels were incinerated, breaking the naval blockade and shattering the besiegers' morale before an agonizing winter of starvation and plague destroyed their army.

60
00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:03.800
[George] Besiegers were reduced to eating horses, camels and leather harnesses in the freezing mud outside the walls.

61
00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:10.040
[George] Of the 1800 vessels that had sailed north, only five battered ships returned to the Levant.

62
00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:24.920
[George] Two centuries later, in 941 AD, a massive fleet of over 1,000 Viking longships, carrying tens of thousands of fierce Ruse warriors under Prince Igor of Kiev, rode down the Black Sea to pillage the imperial capital.

63
00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:35.560
[George] The Imperial Navy was deployed elsewhere, leaving Constantinople defended by only fifteen obsolete reserve galleys hastily fitted with bow and broadside siphons.

64
00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:44.200
[George] The Ruse warriors, renowned for their fearless shield wall tactics, were completely helpless against continuous streams of clinging liquid fire.

65
00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:51.880
[George] Surviving ruse chronicles recorded in terror that the Greeks possessed lightning in their hands, hurling fire that burned upon the waves.

66
00:09:51.880 --> 00:10:01.560
[George] Hundreds of longships were burned to the waterline, and thousands of Viking warriors drowned, cementing the legendary reputation of the Byzantine superweapon.

67
00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:11.960
[George] Recognizing that Greek fire was the sole technological advantage preventing the destruction of the Roman state, the Imperial Court guarded its formula with fanatical paranoia.

68
00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:25.480
[George] In his 10th century treatise on the administration of the empire, Emperor Constantine Vi Porphyrogenitus instructed his son that the secret recipe had been delivered directly to Constantine the Great by an angel of God.

69
00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:39.800
[George] The emperor commanded that any official, noble, or general who dared reveal the secret to a foreign monarch was to be stripped of all titles, proclaimed an enemy of the Roman people, and cursed with eternal damnation at the last judgment.

70
00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:44.040
[George] The manufacture of the weapon was governed by extreme compartmentalization.

71
00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:54.240
[George] The crude petroleum distillers in the Crimea, the sulfur miners, the resin merchants, and the bronze pump makers worked in complete isolation and never met.

72
00:10:54.240 --> 00:11:05.440
[George] No single individual in the entire empire possessed the complete formula and mechanical blueprints, except the reigning emperor and the direct hereditary descendants of Kalinikos's family.

73
00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:11.040
[George] This fanatical secrecy proved to be both the weapon's salvation and its ultimate undoing.

74
00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:25.920
[George] When the rogue armies of the Fourth Crusade sacked Constantinople in 1204, burning the imperial libraries and destroying the state arsenals, the master craftsmen were slaughtered, and the complete formula vanished into the ashes of the burning city.

75
00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:39.360
[George] For 500 years, a single closely held chemical secret had preserved the Eastern Roman Empire, standing as the unyielding fortress wall of medieval Europe until its mystery was lost forever to time.

76
00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:45.440
[Rebecca] The mystery of Greek fire showcases the immense power of technological secrecy in geopolitics.

77
00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:55.520
[Rebecca] Without Kalinicos' terrifying flame, Constantinople would have fallen eight centuries earlier, completely rewriting the religious, political, and cultural history of Europe.

78
00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:58.560
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

79
00:11:58.560 --> 00:11:59.840
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

80
00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:03.280
[Rebecca] Join us next time for another forgotten chapter of history.

81
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:05.600
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

